    "verdict": "approve",
    "findings": [
      {
        "severity": "minor",
        "file": "agent-orchestration/topology/lib/orch-transport.mjs",
        "line": 455,
        "claim": "touchFallback is an unlocked read-modify-write of the host-wide transport.json, now run by a periodic heartbeat in every fallback holder. A heartbeat that read the state before another process recorded recovered_at writes the outage back open, which delays the recovery mail and leaves doctor flagging NATS_CONFIGURED_UNREACHABLE.",
        "evidence": "touchFallback reads with readTransportState and later writes {...state, outage:{...outage, last_fallback_at}} through writeTransportState with no lock or compare. Each fallback transport runs it from setInterval at retireAfterMs/4, concurrently with recordTransportSelection in other processes, which is the writer that sets recovered_at. The existing comment already notes the missing lock for first fallbacks; the heartbeat makes such races routine.",
        "fix": "Re-read immediately before writing and skip if outage.since, recovered_at or the file mtime changed, or do the write under the same lockfile helper used elsewhere (withLock on a transport.json.lock)."
      },
      {
        "severity": "note",
        "file": "agent-orchestration/tests/unit/nats-outage.test.mjs",
        "line": 251,
        "claim": "All three earlier minors are fixed with targeted tests: the sub-quantum or empty b64 payload waits, a closed payload stops the rejoin, and fallback holders refresh via a heartbeat. The request includes no test-run output, and the new outage test is timing-based (800 ms bound) and skips without a nats-server binary."
      }
    ],
